st_has_generated": null, "main_agent_searched": false, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": 1000000, "model_max_tokens": 128000, "output_tail": "Next I'll add a regression test with boundary cases, including negatives (half-up here means away from zero, matching `ROUND_HALF_UP`).\n`round_money(2.675)` now returns `2.68`.\n\n**Cause:** `2.675` can't be stored exactly as a float; it's held as `2.67499999999999982\u2026`. `round()` was also using banker's rounding (ties go to the even digit), not the half-up rule the docstring promises. Before the fix, `round(2.675, 2)` gave `2.67`, `round(1.005, 2)` gave `1.0` and `round(0.125, 2)` gave `0.12`.\n\n**Fix** in `pricing/money.py`: `round_money` now turns the number into the decimal you'd see when printing it (`repr`, so `2.675` becomes `\"2.675\"`). It rounds that with `Decimal.quantize(Decimal(\"0.01\"), ROUND_HALF_UP)` and returns a float as before. Negative halves round away from zero, so `-2.675` gives `-2.68`.\n\n**Test:** I added `tests/test_round_money.py` covering `2.675`, `1.005`, `0.125`, `2.674`, `-2.675` and `10`. pytest isn't installed here, so I checked the same cases with a direct `python3` run instead. All pass, the existing `test_split` still passes, and `add_vat(10)` gives `12.0`.\n\n**One limit:** if a price comes out of a float calculation that lands just below a half (e.g. a product that prints as `2.6749999999999998`), it still rounds down, because that really is the float's value. This can happen with `add_vat`'s `x * (1 + rate)`. The full fix would be doing the money maths in `Decimal` from start to finish; I haven't made that change.", "paced_wait_s": 0.0, "permission_rejections": 0, "read_calls": 2, "replies_ok": 11, "replies_throttled": 0, "requests_cache_read": 309837, "requests_cache_write": 13333, "requests_cost_usd": 0.1837884, "requests_count": 11, "requests_input_uncached": 24, "requests_output": 2753, "reread": false, "rule_order": "none", "rule_prefix": "neither", "run_id